digraph FactoObserve {
    graph [goal="Capture an observation about this repo's product code as a new or updated GitHub Issue"]
    rankdir=LR

    start [shape=Mdiamond, label="Start"]
    exit  [shape=Msquare, label="Exit"]

    // Input: the skill asks the developer for the observation in natural language.
    observation [
        shape=hexagon,
        label="What did you observe? Give a short summary plus any relevant context (source, area or module, and the run, PR, or session it came from).",
        question_type="freeform"
    ]

    // Setup: resolve the tracker repo, gh auth, OKRs path, and project board.
    // Exits non-zero when the repo or gh auth cannot be resolved, which routes
    // straight to the report node so the failure is surfaced to the caller.
    setup [
        label="Resolve Tracker Config",
        shape=parallelogram,
        max_retries=0,
        script="set +e; HOST_ROOT=$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel 2>/dev/null); if [ -z \"$HOST_ROOT\" ]; then echo 'ERROR: not inside a git repository'; exit 1; fi; REPO_SLUG=''; if command -v facto-helper.sh >/dev/null 2>&1; then REPO_SLUG=$(facto-helper.sh tracker.field repo 2>/dev/null); fi; if [ -z \"$REPO_SLUG\" ] || [ \"$REPO_SLUG\" = 'null' ]; then REPO_SLUG=$(gh repo view --json nameWithOwner -q .nameWithOwner 2>/dev/null); fi; if [ -z \"$REPO_SLUG\" ]; then echo 'ERROR: could not derive REPO_SLUG from facto-helper.sh tracker.field repo or gh repo view'; exit 1; fi; if ! gh auth status >/dev/null 2>&1; then echo 'ERROR: gh CLI is not authenticated. Run: gh auth login'; exit 1; fi; echo \"REPO_SLUG=$REPO_SLUG\"; OKRS_PATH_RAW=$(jq -r '.okrs_path // empty' \"$HOST_ROOT/.facto/settings.json\" 2>/dev/null); OKRS_PATH=''; case \"$OKRS_PATH_RAW\" in '') : ;; /*) OKRS_PATH=$OKRS_PATH_RAW ;; *) OKRS_PATH=$HOST_ROOT/$OKRS_PATH_RAW ;; esac; if [ -n \"$OKRS_PATH\" ] && [ -f \"$OKRS_PATH\" ]; then echo 'OKRS_AVAILABLE=true'; echo \"OKRS_PATH=$OKRS_PATH\"; else echo 'OKRS_AVAILABLE=false'; fi; PROJECT_CHECK=''; if command -v facto-helper.sh >/dev/null 2>&1; then PROJECT_CHECK=$(facto-helper.sh tracker.field project 2>/dev/null); fi; if [ -z \"$PROJECT_CHECK\" ] || [ \"$PROJECT_CHECK\" = 'null' ]; then echo 'PROJECT_AVAILABLE=false'; else echo 'PROJECT_AVAILABLE=true'; echo \"PROJECT_OWNER=$(facto-helper.sh tracker.field project.owner)\"; echo \"PROJECT_NUMBER=$(facto-helper.sh tracker.field project.number)\"; echo \"PROJECT_NAME=$(facto-helper.sh tracker.field project.name)\"; echo \"STATUS_FIELD_NAME=$(facto-helper.sh tracker.field status_field)\"; echo \"STATUS_BACKLOG_NAME=$(facto-helper.sh tracker.field status_values.backlog)\"; echo \"STATUS_DONE_NAME=$(facto-helper.sh tracker.field status_values.done)\"; fi; echo '--- issue templates ---'; ls .github/ISSUE_TEMPLATE 2>/dev/null || echo 'none'"
    ]

    // Phase 1: classify the observation and draft the body sections.
    draft [
        label="Classify and Draft",
        max_visits=3,
        prompt="Classify the observation and draft the Issue body. Use the developer's freeform answer from the preceding human gate (run context key `human.gate.text`) as the raw input, together with any metadata they gave: `source` (one of `developer-feedback`, `claude-code-logs`, `internet-research`, or another short string; default `developer-feedback`), `related-project`, `related-area`, and `related-run`. Those all flow inline into the Issue body, never into frontmatter.\n\nCLASSIFY as exactly one of:\n- BUG: something is not working right — a reproducible failure, regression, slowness, or drift. Default when `source: claude-code-logs`.\n- FEATURE: a capability that does not exist yet and would help. Default when the input frames `we should have X` or `X is missing`.\n- CHORE: cleanup — refactor, dependency bump, docs maintenance, consolidation of drift.\nWhen the input is `source: internet-research`, there is no prefix and the voice is third-party, but the body shape follows the BUG structure.\nIf BUG vs FEATURE is genuinely ambiguous, prefer BUG when there is a concrete failure mode to capture and FEATURE when the framing is forward-looking.\n\nDRAFT THE SECTIONS. First check whether the host repo has `.github/ISSUE_TEMPLATE/1-bug.md` or its feature/chore equivalent (the setup step listed the directory). If a matching template exists, read it and use its canonical section names and heading levels. If not, use this fallback structure and note that the fallback fired.\n\nBUG fallback: `## What's happening`, `### Impact`, `## Repro Steps`, `### Observed Result`, `### Expected Result`, `## Root cause(s)`.\nFEATURE fallback: `## What's missing`, `## Why it would help`, `## Sketch`, `## Open questions`.\nCHORE fallback: `## What needs cleaning up`, `## Desired end state`, `## Open questions`.\n\nSection guidance. What's happening: one paragraph, the event anchored in time, quoting evidence briefly where useful. Impact: one paragraph naming the concrete effect — time lost, quality miss, regression, developer correction; for a positive observation name what improved instead. If the setup step reported `OKRS_AVAILABLE=true`, read the OKRs file at the printed path and frame Impact in KR terms when the observation moves one; otherwise write Impact with no OKR references. Repro Steps / Observed Result / Expected Result: include all three only when the observation is a reproducible failure, and omit all three entirely (no empty headings) for enhancement-style observations, research findings, or one-off events. When you do include them, write the shortest path that reproduces the high-level issue, not a transcript of what you happened to do — and if the failure surfaced inside a tool run by a skill, the repro is `run the skill` with its minimal triggering inputs, not the individual command the skill invoked. Root cause(s): a bullet list where each bullet names something an improvement could change (a skill prompt, a hook, a missing check); mark a genuinely uncertain cause `(uncertain)` rather than fabricating a chain.\n\nAlso decide the STANCE, which later phases need: negative when the observation is a recurrence or a new failure, positive when it reports that a previously broken thing now works.\n\nDRAFT THE TITLE using the rule for the classified type. BUG: state the problem, not a fix, because the same problem may attract several fix attempts and the title must outlive them (`BUG: upload button hangs on files larger than 5MB`, not `Add file-size check to upload handler`). FEATURE: name the desired capability, not its absence (`FEATURE: Add bulk-export capability to the reporting screen`). CHORE: name the cleanup needed, not the past mistake (`CHORE: Consolidate duplicate auth-token helpers`). Internet-research: no prefix, third-party voice.\n\nDo not put any fix recommendation in the body — this skill files what happened, not what to do about it. Output the classification, the stance, the title, and the full drafted body."
    ]

    // Phase 6: the 'when to ask' triggers, as a single explicit checkpoint.
    clarify [
        shape=hexagon,
        label="Draft ready. Continue to matching, or send it back for a revision? (Send it back if a root cause is marked uncertain, the input was too thin to draft a section, or the classification looks wrong.)"
    ]

    // Phase 2: one bulk fetch of candidate Issues, with pagination.
    fetch_candidates [
        label="Fetch Candidate Issues",
        prompt="Bulk-fetch the candidate Issues in one call. Never loop `gh issue view` per Issue — the bulk fetch is the performance lever.\n\nUse the REPO_SLUG printed by the setup step:\n\n    THIRTY_DAYS_AGO=$(date -d '30 days ago' +%Y-%m-%d)\n    gh -R $REPO_SLUG issue list --state all --search \"is:issue updated:>=$THIRTY_DAYS_AGO\" --json number,title,state,stateReason,body,labels,comments,closedAt,updatedAt,projectItems --limit 200\n\nIf exactly 200 records come back, paginate: set the upper bound of `updated:` to the oldest `updatedAt` in the page you just received and repeat until fewer than 200 return. Concatenate the pages locally.\n\nFilter locally: keep every OPEN Issue regardless of `updatedAt`, and every CLOSED Issue whose `closedAt` is within the last 30 days. Drop closed Issues older than 30 days even if they were updated recently — that is just a stray comment.\n\nIf the setup step reported `PROJECT_AVAILABLE=true`, annotate each candidate with its project Status. `projectItems` entries look like `{\"status\":{\"optionId\":\"...\",\"name\":\"Backlog\"},\"title\":\"<project name>\"}`, so the human-readable option name is at `.status.name`. Collect matches into an array and take `first` before falling back, because jq rejects `//` placed directly after a parenthesized expression inside an object value:\n\n    jq --arg name \"$PROJECT_NAME\" '[.[] | . + {projectStatus: (([.projectItems[] | select(.title == $name) | .status.name] | first) // null)}]'\n\nIf `status` is absent from the inline data on this `gh` version, fall back to `gh project item-list $PROJECT_NUMBER --owner $PROJECT_OWNER --format json --limit 200` and merge by issue number. A `null` Status is acceptable — later phases treat it as `no Status known`. When `PROJECT_AVAILABLE=false`, skip Status extraction entirely.\n\nOutput the filtered candidate list: number, title, state, closedAt, project Status, plus each candidate's body and its most recent comments (up to ~30) so the next step can judge the match without re-fetching. Also report how many candidates survived the filter."
    ]

    // Phase 3: same-root-cause judgement.
    match [
        label="Judge Root-Cause Match",
        shape=tab,
        fidelity="full",
        prompt="For each candidate Issue in the context above, read its body and its most recent comments and decide whether it shares the ROOT CAUSE of the new observation — not merely the symptom.\n\nThe trap: two components can both hang, both produce empty output, both fail at commit time, for entirely unrelated reasons. Matching on symptom alone silently merges distinct problems. Match only when the actionable cause underneath the symptom is the same. When in doubt, treat it as no-match and let the new Issue carry a possible-duplicate note for human triage.\n\nProduce exactly one verdict:\n- `no-match`: no candidate shares the root cause.\n- `single-match`: exactly one candidate clearly shares the root cause. Record its number, its state (open or closed), its `closedAt` if closed, and its project Status.\n- `ambiguous-match`: several candidates partially fit but none clearly. Record every candidate number with a one-line rationale each.\n\nState the verdict, the evidence for it, and the number of candidates you considered."
    ]

    // Phase 4: the decision table.
    decide [
        label="Decide the Action",
        shape=tab,
        prompt="Apply the decision table to the stance from the draft step and the match verdict from the previous step. Output exactly one action label plus the target Issue number where applicable.\n\n- negative + no-match: 5a — create a new Issue.\n- negative + ambiguous-match: 5a — create a new Issue with a possible-duplicate note.\n- negative + single-match, Issue open: 5b — comment with the new evidence.\n- negative + single-match, Issue closed: 5c — reopen and comment with the new evidence.\n- positive + no-match: drop. A positive observation with no Issue to confirm carries nothing the system can act on. Report and stop.\n- positive + ambiguous-match: 5a — create a new Issue with a possible-duplicate note, so the positive signal is not lost.\n- positive + single-match, open, HIGH confidence: 5d — close as completed with the evidence as the closing comment.\n- positive + single-match, open, LOWER confidence: 5f — comment only, no state change.\n- positive + single-match, closed: drop. The positive evidence only reinforces a settled outcome.\n\nConfidence cut for the autonomous close (5d). HIGH requires all of: the positive observation directly demonstrates the original failure mode — as described in the Issue body and its supporting comments — now succeeding; the action took place under conditions similar to the original failures; and no contradicting evidence exists in the Issue's recent comments. LOWER is anything partial or indirect: an adjacent area behaving correctly rather than the exact scenario, an Issue whose root-cause description is broader than what the observation covers, or any contradiction in recent comments.\n\nWhen in doubt choose LOWER. 5f is a comment with no state change; 5d closes the Issue and is much heavier to reverse. Never close on a single ambiguous positive observation."
    ]

    // Phase 5: execute via gh.
    execute [
        label="Execute via gh",
        prompt="Execute the action chosen in the previous step. Every `gh` call passes `-R $REPO_SLUG` so it targets the right repo regardless of cwd. Every Issue body and every comment body starts with `observe says: `; that provenance prefix is how skill-authored content is told apart from developer-authored content when both run as the same GitHub user. Get the date from the shell at invocation time with `date +%Y-%m-%d` — never hardcode one. Wrap every `gh project` call in a check that the setup step reported `PROJECT_AVAILABLE=true`; skip Status writes silently otherwise.\n\nIf the decision was `drop`, do nothing and say so.\n\n5a — CREATE A NEW ISSUE. Assemble the body from the sections drafted earlier, honoring the omit-if-N/A rule for Repro Steps / Observed Result / Expected Result. If the verdict was `ambiguous-match`, insert a `## Possible duplicate` section before the footer reading: `This Issue may be the same root cause as #N (one-line reason) and/or #M (one-line reason). Surfacing here rather than commenting on a wrong Issue. Human triage welcome.` Always append a horizontal rule and the line `_observe says: filed YYYY-MM-DD from source=<source>; area=<related-area or n/a>; run=<related-run or n/a>._` Then run `gh -R $REPO_SLUG issue create --title '<PREFIX>: <one-line statement>' --body \"$BODY\"`, where PREFIX is BUG, FEATURE, or CHORE (omitted entirely for `source: internet-research`). Print the resulting Issue URL. When a project is configured, add the new Issue to it with `gh project item-add` and set its Status to the configured backlog value with `gh project item-edit --project-id $PROJECT_ID --id $ITEM_ID --field-id $STATUS_FIELD_ID --single-select-option-id $STATUS_BACKLOG_ID`. Resolve PROJECT_ID via `gh project view` and the Status field and option IDs via `gh project field-list` at run time — never hardcode node IDs, since they change if the project is recreated.\n\n5b — COMMENT ON AN OPEN MATCHED ISSUE (negative). `gh -R $REPO_SLUG issue comment <n> --body ...` with: `observe says: seen again <date>.`, then `**What happened:**` one-line event summary with the run reference if available, `**Impact:**` one-line concrete effect, and `**Same root cause as this Issue:** yes — <one-line confirmation>`. No Status change.\n\n5c — REOPEN AND COMMENT (recurrence on a closed Issue). Run `gh -R $REPO_SLUG issue reopen <n>`, set Status to the backlog value (recurrence means the previous fix did not hold, so it is fresh work), then comment with the same shape as 5b plus `**State change:** reopened — problem has recurred after the earlier close.` and a note of the date it had been closed.\n\n5d — CLOSE AS COMPLETED (high-confidence positive). Use the full comment template, not a one-liner: `gh -R $REPO_SLUG issue close <n> --reason completed --comment ...` with `observe says: closing as completed <date>.`, `**What happened:**` demonstrating the original failure mode now succeeding, `**Impact:**` stating the original problem no longer reproduces, `**Same root cause as this Issue:** yes — <confirmation>`, `**State change:** closed as completed — positive evidence confirms the original failure mode now succeeds. If this is wrong, reopen and add evidence.`, and a `**PR link:**` note asking that the implementing PR's description carry `Resolves #<n>`. Then set Status to the configured done value.\n\n5f — COMMENT ONLY (lower-confidence positive). Same comment shape as 5b, ending with `**State change:** no state change — positive evidence noted but not yet sufficient to close.` No Status change.\n\nThe `Same root cause` line is always present on 5b, 5c, 5d, and 5f — it is what makes the match decision auditable. Report the exact commands you ran and their output."
    ]

    // Phase 7: report.
    report [
        label="Report",
        shape=tab,
        fidelity="summary:high",
        prompt="Report the outcome in a single line plus the Issue URL, using only what actually happened above.\n\nShape it like these examples:\n- `Created new Issue: <url> (Status: Backlog) (no match found among 18 candidates)`\n- `Commented on existing Issue #51: <url> (no Status change) (recurrence — same root cause)`\n- `Closed Issue #58 as completed: <url> (Status: Done) (high-confidence positive evidence)`\n- `Reopened Issue #44: <url> (Status: Backlog) (recurrence)`\n- `Created new Issue #82 with possible-duplicate note (uncertain match against #51, #58): <url> (Status: Backlog)`\n\nAppend a parenthetical for each degradation that fired: `(no project board configured; status writes skipped)`, `(no OKRs configured; OKR framing skipped)`, `(no Issue templates found; used built-in section shape)`. These are supported operating modes, not errors.\n\nIf the setup step failed, report its exact error instead and state that no Issue was filed. If the decision was `drop`, say which drop rule applied.\n\nAlso return, for a calling workflow to record: the action label (`created`, `commented`, `closed`, `commented-positive`, `created-possible-duplicate`, or `dropped`), the resulting Issue number, and the resulting Status value or `no-change`."
    ]

    start -> observation
    observation -> setup [freeform=true]
    setup -> draft [condition="outcome=succeeded"]
    setup -> report
    draft -> clarify
    clarify -> fetch_candidates [label="[C] Continue to matching"]
    clarify -> draft [label="[R] Revise the draft"]
    fetch_candidates -> match
    match -> decide
    decide -> execute
    execute -> report
    report -> exit
}
